From: ahmedawachi <121810925+ahmedawachi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:33:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] cygwin: report real process and system memory through
 Win32

What: Cygwin/MSYS2 builds only (HAVE_WIN32_MEMINFO, set in config.h when
__CYGWIN__ is defined; the Makefile adds the object when uname -s starts
with CYGWIN or MSYS, and adds nothing anywhere else).
- New src/win32_meminfo.c/.h. The .c file includes only <windows.h>,
  <psapi.h> and its own header, and exposes plain-C functions:
  win32GetProcessMemory() (K32GetProcessMemoryInfo: PrivateUsage,
  WorkingSetSize) and win32GetSystemMemory() (K32GetPerformanceInfo:
  CommitTotal, CommitLimit, PhysicalAvailable, each multiplied by PageSize;
  it falls back to GlobalMemoryStatusEx if GetPerformanceInfo fails).
  PSAPI_VERSION 2 selects the kernel32 K32* exports, which the Cygwin and
  MSYS2 gcc drivers already link (-lkernel32), so no import library is
  added. Neither function calls malloc.
- zmalloc_get_rss() returns the working set. Before, it returned
  zmalloc_used_memory(), so used_memory_rss, mem_fragmentation_ratio and
  the allocator_* ratios were fake (every ratio read 1.00).
- zmalloc_get_private_dirty(-1), which only the fork child calls, returns
  the child's PrivateUsage. The child's log line now reads "Fork private
  copy for RDB: ... (Cygwin fork is not copy-on-write)" instead of
  "Fork CoW ... 0 MB". rdb_last_cow_size and the related INFO fields now
  carry that figure on these builds.
- INFO memory gains process_private_bytes, process_working_set,
  system_commit_total and system_commit_limit, read live on each INFO
  (0 = unavailable). They are not sampled in serverCron.

Why: a production crash of the MSYS2 build was Windows refusing a commit, and none of the numbers
that would have shown commit pressure were visible. It deliberately does
not define HAVE_PROC_STAT or read /proc/self/stat: Cygwin's field 24 counts 4 KB pages while
sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE) is 64 KB, so RSS would read 16x too high. statm is
not read either: it walks the whole working-set list with malloc.

Risk: low. One Win32 call per 100 ms cron sample for the working set
(ProcessVmCounters, cheap). GetPerformanceInfo is heavier (it counts every
process's handles and threads) and runs only when INFO memory is
requested. Whether the w32api headers ship with MSYS2 "gcc" and Cygwin
"gcc-core" is verified by the CI build, not here. The working set
includes shared DLL pages and can be trimmed by Windows, so
mem_fragmentation_ratio can now read below 1.0.
